{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788163023,"nonce":"JG9kXxFic8Vx1cwCdHAruHWCILI=","message":"Fixed in bd6eaea on `docs/ci-simplification`.\n\n`.github/path-filters.yml` holds ONE definition of the filters, read by both\n`ci.yml` and `image.yml` (602940c). `ci.yml` grows a `changes` job emitting\n`rust` / `ui` / `coverage` / `contracts`; every other job is gated on it, and\nthe four newly-skippable checks moved to `required-if-run` in the same commit.\n\nThree deviations from the plan in the handoff:\n\n1. `api` became `contracts` and INCLUDES `ui`. `openapi-drift`'s second half\n   runs `npm run gen \u0026\u0026 npm run typecheck`, making it the only TypeScript\n   typecheck in CI — gating it on Rust alone would have dropped tsc from every\n   UI PR. Its 0.8 min was not waste.\n2. A push to MAIN filters nothing. Trunk is what every build and every `sha-`\n   image derives from; the minutes were being burnt on PR pushes anyway.\n3. A new `required ci/changes`. With everything else skippable, a docs-only PR\n   would otherwise pass `just pr-gate` having validated literally nothing, and\n   a broken filter expression would report green. `changes` always runs, so it\n   is the anchor.\n\nPrerequisite fixed in the same commit: `pr_gate.py` treated bucket `skipping`\nas acceptable at ANY tier, so an `if:`-skipped `required` check passed the\ngate. Filters without that fix would have made the whole `required-if-run` tier\ndecorative. Skipped and missing are now the same verdict; the tier decides.\n\n`coverage`'s filter also includes `scripts/coverage_ratchet.py` and\n`docs/audits/coverage-baseline.toml` — a baseline edit with no Rust change is\nexactly the case that must re-run it, or a lowered floor merges unvalidated.\n\nVerified: actionlint clean on all workflows, and the four gate scenarios\n(UI-only / docs-only / changes-job-absent / red Rust suite) exit 0/0/1/1.","files":null}]}